ld automatically record events and timestamps across prototypes, meeting Article 12's traceability requirement. Score 4.\n\nnovelty paradigm_relatedness (1-4):\n4 = paradigm breaking introduces new elements AND changes relationship between lab's R&D and compliance obligations, or addresses larger problem of what lab researches\n3 = paradigm stretching changes that relationship (e.g., logging woven into science, new partners...)\n2 = slightly paradigm stretching: introduces new elements (tools, formats, processes) but still treats compliance as logging practice\n1 = paradigm preserving: standard compliance and logging practice; lab's way unchanged\n\nUnified library is a tool/process for compliance. Still treats compliance as logging practice. Introduces new elements tools/processes. So slightly paradigm stretching: 2.\n\nEvidence: The proposal adds a reusable logging tool but keeps compliance as separate logging practice rather than reshaping R&D-compliance relationship. Score 2.\n\noriginality (1-4):\n4 = not expressed before AND ingenious...\n3 = unusual interesting shows some imagination\n2 = interesting\n1 = common mundane boring\n\nUnified logging library for AI compliance is fairly common idea. Might be common, mundane. Could be interesting but common. Maybe 1 or 2. Standardized software library plug-and-play logging is common practice. I'd say 1 = common, mundane, boring.\n\nEvidence: Centralized logging libraries are a common engineering practice for regulatory compliance in labs. Score 1.\n\nWORKABILITY\nacceptability (1-4):\n4 = common strategies that violate no norms...\nProposal is acceptable. Secure immutable ledger, wrappers. Acceptable. Score 4.\n\nEvidence: Building an internal logging library complies with legal requirements and does not offend sensibilities. Score 4.\n\nimplementability (1-4):\n4 = easy to implement at low cost, or non-radical changes\n3 = some changes, or reasonably feasible measures\n2 = significant change, or expensive...\nUnifi