radigm stretching changes that relationship (e.g., logging woven into science, new partners...). 2 = slightly paradigm stretching: introduces new elements (tools formats processes) but still treats compliance as logging practice. 1 = paradigm preserving standard compliance and logging practice; lab's way unchanged.\n\nProposal introduces living repository linking code changes to docs, dashboard flags missing metadata. That's new tools/processes but still treats compliance as logging practice? It is about documentation, not logging. Slightly paradigm stretching: introduces new elements but still treats compliance as logging practice. So 2.\n\nNeed evidence sentence per subscale.\n\nProvide output with id and each subscale_evidence then subscale.\n\nProbably format:\n\nid: P1633\nacceptability_evidence: ...\nacceptability: 4\n...\n\nMake sure one sentence each.\n\nLet's craft sentences concise.\n\nAcceptability evidence: The pipeline automates documentation upkeep using existing version control and a dashboard, which aligns with regulatory expectations and offends no norms. Score 4.\n\nImplementability evidence: Integrating version control with a compliance dashboard requires custom tooling and metadata mapping but builds on tools labs already use, making it reasonably feasible. Score 3.\n\nApplicability evidence: The proposal targets up-to-date technical documentation linked to code and data changes, which is an implied traceability need related to the logging and record-keeping mandate. Score 3.\n\nEffectiveness evidence: Keeping dossiers current helps audit readiness and post-market monitoring but does not provide automatic event logs required by Article 12, so it only partially addresses the mandate. Score 3.\n\nOriginality evidence: Linking commits and dataset updates to compliance metadata in real time is an interesting workflow improvement but resembles common DevOps-for-compliance practices. Score 2.\n\nParadigm_relatedness evidence: The system adds new docume