ially legally politically acceptable.\nCommon strategies that violate no norms => 4. It's just documentation. Acceptable.\n\neffectiveness (1-4): degree to which idea will solve problem.\nProblem: decide how to respond to logging mandate and/or adapt R&D accordingly. Approval means committing scarce implementation resources to pursue proposal, not funding to explore whether idea might someday work. Dynamic portal addresses technical documentation per Recital 71, not Article 12 logging of events/logs? It mentions capabilities/limitations reports, prevents documentation lag, facilitates audits during post-market monitoring. Does it solve logging mandate? Logging mandate is automatic recording of events logs over lifetime, recording period of each use, reference database, etc. Documentation portal is related but not directly logging mandate. It's about technical documentation. Might contribute partially. Effectiveness: reasonable and will contribute to solution of problem (it helps, but only partial) => 3. Could be 4? Problem stated: must decide how to meet logging mandate and/or adapt R&D accordingly. This proposal addresses documentation not logging events. So partial.\n\napplicability (1-4): degree to which idea clearly applies to stated problem.\nSolves an identified problem that is directly related to stated problem (do X to get Y, and Y is part of stated problem) => 4? The stated problem includes meeting logging mandate and/or adapt R&D. Documentation portal is about traceability and compliance verification per Recital 71 which is linked. Might be directly related? Article 12 is record-keeping logs. Recital 71 mentions technical documentation. The proposal cites Recital 71. So applies.\n\nMaybe applicability 4 = solves identified problem directly related. It facilitates audits during post-market monitoring phase. That's part of compliance. I'd say 3 = solves implied problem related. Hmm.\n\nThe proposal explicitly references Recital 71, not Article 12 logs. The mandate is Article 12 logging. Documentation portal is different. Might be implied problem: documentation lag. Could be applicability 3.\n\nLet's decide: applicability = 3? Let's think: Stated problem: must decide how to meet logging mandate and/or adapt R&D accordingly. Proposal is about technical documentation portal, not logging events. So it may have some benefit within special situation and somehow relates. Might be 2.\n\nHmm instrument: applicability 4 = solves an identified problem that is directly related to the stated problem (do X to get Y, and Y is part of stated problem). 3 = solves an implied problem that is related.\n\nThe problem mentions logging mandate. Documentation portal is about documentation, not logs. But Recital 71 is quoted in problem. Might be considered related.\n\nProbably applicability = 3 (solves implied problem related to compliance). Effectiveness = 3 (contributes).\n\nLet's output.\n\nNeed format: for each subscale give <subscale>_evidence sentence then <subscale> integer.\n\nProvide JSON? Probably just lines.\n\nReturn rating echoing id exactly.\n\nI'll produce:\n\nid: P1416\noriginality_evidence: ...\noriginality: 3\netc.\n\nEnsure one sentence evidence.\n\nLet's craft sentences.\n\noriginality_evidence: The version-controlled wiki linked to Git for auto-updating capabilities reports is a familiar DevOps practice applied to AI compliance, making it unusual and interesting but not ingenious or surprising. => score 3\n\nparadigm_relatedness_evidence: It introduces new documentation tools and processes but still treats compliance as an internal logging and documentation practice rather than changing the lab’s R&D-compliance relationship. => score 2\n\nimplementability_evidence: Integrating a wiki with existing Git repositories to auto-generate reports requires some development and process changes but is reasonably feasible for an R&D lab. => score 3\n\nacceptability_evidence: Centralizing technical documentation for regulatory audits violates no legal, social or business norms and is a common compliance strategy. => score 4\n\neffectiveness_evidence: The portal improves traceability and audit readiness for post-market monitoring, but it does not create the automatic event logs required by Article 12(1)-(3). => score 3\n\napplicability_evidence: The proposal addresses Recital 71 documentation needs that are implied by the broader compliance problem of meeting the AI Act’s record-keeping obligations. => score 3\n\nReturn.\n\n",
