sting shows some imagination\n2 = interesting\n1 = common mundane boring\n\nPerson identification logging for Annex III is explicitly required by Article 12(3)(d). So common. It's standard. Maybe level 1 = common, mundane, boring.\n\nEvidence: Identifying verifying personnel with authentication and timestamp is the literal requirement of Article 12(3)(d) and a common audit control.\n\neffectiveness (1-4):\n4 = reasonable and will solve stated problem without regard for workability\n3 = reasonable and will contribute to solution\n2 = unreasonable...\n1 = solves unrelated problem\n\nWill solve logging mandate for person identification. Does it solve overall logging mandate? It addresses only paragraph 3(d) for Annex III systems. The stated problem: must decide how to meet logging mandate and/or adapt R&D accordingly. This proposal directly satisfies stringent person-identification requirements. Partial solution? Might be partial. Effectiveness definition: degree to which idea will solve problem. It solves part of logging mandate. Probably reasonable and will contribute to solution, not fully solve all logging (period of use, reference database, input data). So level 3.\n\nEvidence: It ensures traceability for verification personnel as required but does not address the other logging elements mandated by Article 12.\n\napplicability (1-4):\n4 = solves identified problem directly related to stated problem\n3 = solves implied problem related\n2 = may have some benefit within special situation...\n1 = ...\n\nIt directly applies to logging mandate, specifically person identification. So level 4.\n\nEvidence: The proposal maps directly onto Article 12(3)(d)’s requirement to identify natural persons involved in verification.\n\nacceptability (1-4):\n4 = common strategies that violate no norms\n3 = somewhat uncommon...\n2 = offends sensibilities somewhat\n1 = radically violates...\n\nAuthentication gateway logging user ID is acceptable. Probably common strategies violate no norms. Level 4.\n\nEvidence: Mandatory authentication and immutable audit trails are standard, legally compliant practices with no normative conflict.\n\nimplementability (1-4):\n4 = easy to implement at low cost, or non-radical changes\n3 = some changes, or reasonably feasible measures\n2 = significant change, expensive...\n1 = totally infeasible\n\nImplementing authentication gateway at final verification step: some changes, reasonably feasible. Could be 3? Might be easy? Requires integration into workflow. I'd say some changes reasonably feasible => 3.\n\nEvidence: Adding a gateway and logging module requires workflow integration and identity infrastructure but is a reasonably feasible engineering change.\n\nReturn rating echo id.\n\nNeed format: For each subscale give <subscale>_evidence sentence then <subscale> integer.\n\nProbably JSON? Instruction says return rating echoing its id exactly. For each subscale... give \"<subscale>_evidence\" first and then \"<subscale>\". Likely output list.\n\nI'll output structured.\n\nLet's produce:\n\nid: P2138\nparadigm_relatedness_evidence: ...\nparadigm_relatedness: 2\n...\n\nMake sure one sentence evidence.\n\nCheck paradigm_relatedness evidence one sentence. Good.\n\nPotential nuance: paradigm_relatedness could be 1 paradigm preserving standard compliance. The proposal introduces specialized tracking module, mandatory authentication gateway. That's new elements but still treats compliance as logging practice => slightly paradigm stretching =2. Accept.\n\nOriginality 1 common mundane.\n\nEffectiveness 3 contributes partial.\n\nApplicability 4 solves identified problem directly related.\n\nAcceptability 4 common strategies violate no norms.\n\nImplementability 3 some changes reasonably feasible.\n\nReturn.",
