on must decide how to respond: how to meet the logging mandate and/or adapt its R&D accordingly.\n\nProposal is about documentation pipeline, not logging mandate per Article 12. It mentions extracts metadata from training logs... but focus on technical documentation. Logging mandate is automatic recording of events over lifetime. This proposal does not address logging capabilities for identifying risk situations, post-market monitoring etc. So it solves unrelated problem? It solves documentation overhead, which is related to Recital 71. The stated problem includes meet logging mandate and/or adapt R&D. Documentation is part of compliance but not logging.\n\nEffectiveness: maybe 3 = reasonable and will contribute to solution (helps with compliance burden). Even though it's not logging, it contributes to overall compliance. I'd say 3.\n\nApplicability (1-4): degree to which idea clearly applies to stated problem.\n4 = solves identified problem directly related to stated problem (do X to get Y, and Y is part of stated problem)\n3 = solves implied problem related...\nProposal does X = automated docs pipeline to get Y = reduced admin overhead for technical documentation. Is technical documentation part of stated problem? Stated problem is meet logging mandate and/or adapt R&D accordingly. Recital 71 mentions technical documentation needed. Article 12 is logging. The proposal focuses on comprehensible information requirement of Recital 71. That's directly related to compliance. Might be 4? Logging mandate vs documentation. The proposal explicitly says focuses on comprehensible information requirement of Recital 71. The problem statement says must decide how to meet logging mandate and/or adapt R&D. Documentation is not logging mandate but still compliance.\n\nI'd argue applicability = 3 solves implied problem related to stated problem (reduce admin overhead). Or 4? Hmm.\n\nThe anchored level 4: solves an identified problem that is directly related to the stated problem (do X to get Y, and Y is part of the stated problem). Y is reduced paperwork for researchers. Is that part of stated problem? Stated problem is meet logging mandate. Reducing paperwork is not exactly meeting logging mandate. Might be implied.\n\nI'll give applicability 3.\n\nParadigm_relatedness (1-4): degree to which idea preserves or modifies paradigm.\n4 = paradigm breaking: introduces new elements AND changes relationship between lab's R&D and compliance obligations, or addresses larger problem of what lab researches\n3 = paradigm stretching: changes that relationship (e.g., logging woven into science, new partners or offerings born from compliance)\n2 = slightly paradigm stretching: introduces new elements (tools, formats, processes) but still treats compliance as logging practice\n1 = paradigm preserving: standard compliance and logging practice; lab's way of working unchanged\n\nProposal creates toolchain, automated template generator, integrates version control. This is new tools but still treats compliance as documentation practice. Slightly paradigm stretching? Introduces new elements (tools) but still treats compliance as paperwork. So level 2.\n\nOriginality (1-4): degree to which idea is not only rare but also ingenious.\n4 = not expressed before AND ingenious...\n3 = unusual, interesting; shows some imagination\n2 = interesting\n1 = common, mundane, boring\n\nAutomated compliance documentation pipeline extracting metadata to populate technical docs. This is fairly common in ML MLOps. Might be common, mundane. Could be 1 or 2. Probably common practice. I'd say 1 = common, mundane, boring.\n\nImplementability (1-4): degree to which idea can be easily implemented.\n4 = easy to implement at low cost, or non-radical changes\n3 = some changes, or reasonably feasible measures\n2 = significant change, or expensive or difficult...\nProposal integrates version control with automated template generator pulling hyperparameters etc. Some changes, reasonably feasible.