ully solve all logging requirements (needs start/end time, input data for match, identification persons). It focuses on reference database identifier. So partial solution = 3.\n\napplicability (1-4): degree clearly applies to stated problem.\n4 = solves identified problem directly related\n3 = solves implied problem related\nProposal directly addresses record-keeping mandate and traceability. So applicability 4? Solves identified problem that is directly related to stated problem. Yes.\n\nNow produce output with id and subscale evidence then score.\n\nNeed format: echo its id exactly. For each subscale in order, give \"<subscale>_evidence\" first then \"<subscale>\" integer.\n\nProbably JSON? Not specified. Could be lines. Safer to output structured.\n\nI'll output:\n\nid: P1794\nacceptability_evidence: ...\nacceptability: 4\netc.\n\nOne sentence evidence for each rating.\n\nEnsure one sentence.\n\nLet's craft sentences.\n\nacceptability_evidence: The proposal uses standard hashing and immutable storage to secure logs, a common technical safeguard that does not violate legal norms or sensibilities.\nacceptability: 4\n\nimplementability_evidence: Implementing a hashing mechanism and immutable ledger alongside existing logs requires integration work and infrastructure changes but is reasonably feasible for an R&D lab.\nimplementability: 3\n\noriginality_evidence: Linking every inference to exact training and reference database versions via an immutable ledger is unusual and shows imagination beyond basic logging.\noriginality: 3\n\nparadigm_relatedness_evidence: The idea adds new tools and formats for lineage tracking but still frames compliance as a logging practice rather than changing how R&D and compliance relate.\nparadigm_relatedness: 2\n\neffectiveness_evidence: By creating tamper-evident links between outputs and reference data, the system would substantially improve traceability for Annex III systems though it does not alone guarantee all mandated log fields.