s unrelated problem.\n\nThe proposal aims to advance scientific frontier while directly addressing regulatory needs. But it's a research project, not implementation. So it probably will not solve immediate compliance need. Might be partial contribution long term. I'd say 3? The instrument says approval means committing scarce implementation resources to pursue a proposal, not funding to explore whether idea might someday work. So research project is exploration, not implementation. So effectiveness low.\n\nMaybe effectiveness =2: unreasonable or unlikely to solve problem (it probably will not work). Because it doesn't produce logs.\n\nApplicability: degree to which idea clearly applies to stated problem. 4 = solves identified problem directly related. 3 = solves implied problem related. 2 = may have some benefit within special situation and somehow relates. 1 = intervention is not stated or does not produce useful outcome.\n\nThe proposal relates to logging mandate, but focuses on intellectual merit. Might be 3? Solves implied problem of transparency. I'd say applicability =3? It clearly applies to logging mandate: mapping internal states to human-readable logs satisfies comprehensible information requirement. So it does apply. Maybe 3 solves implied problem? Could be 4? The stated problem is how to meet logging mandate and/or adapt R&D accordingly. This proposal adapts R&D. So maybe 4 = solves identified problem directly related to stated problem (do X to get Y, and Y is part of stated problem). It does X = research, Y = metrics for logs. Might be direct.\n\nLet's think: Applicability 3 = solves an implied problem that is related. The stated problem is meet logging mandate. The proposal doesn't meet mandate, it researches standards. That's implied? Hmm.\n\nMaybe applicability =2? May have some benefit within special situation and somehow relates.\n\nBetter interpret: Effectiveness low, applicability maybe 3.\n\nWorkability implementability: degree to which idea can