y needs. That sounds like changes relationship: logging woven into science, maybe paradigm stretching. Might be 3? Or 4? Introduces new elements AND changes relationship between lab's R&D and compliance obligations, or addresses larger problem of what lab researches. It does address larger problem of what lab researches (interpretability). Could be paradigm breaking? Probably paradigm stretching: logging woven into science. I'd say 3.\n\nLet's decide:\n\neffectiveness evidence: The proposal funds long-term research papers rather than implementing logging, so it is unlikely to meet the immediate mandate to record events. Score 2\n\napplicability evidence: It directly targets comprehensible logs required by Recital 71, so it clearly relates to the logging mandate even if indirectly. Score 3? Or 4? Could be 3.\n\nMaybe applicability =3: solves implied problem of transparency. I'll pick 3.\n\nimplementability evidence: Defining metrics and publishing papers is reasonably feasible with existing R&D staff and compute. Score 3\n\nacceptability evidence: Research on transparency and publishing results violates no legal, social or business norms. Score 4\n\noriginality evidence: Formalizing mathematical standards for meaningful logs in latent spaces is unusual and shows imagination beyond standard logging. Score 3\n\nparadigm_relatedness evidence: It weaves compliance-driven traceability into core interpretability research, changing how R&D relates to compliance without breaking the research paradigm. Score 3\n\nProvide output JSON? The prompt says return rating echoing its id exactly. For each subscale... give \"<subscale>_evidence\" first and then \"<subscale>\". Probably JSON object.\n\nI'll output with id P2429 and fields.\n\nEnsure order listed: effectiveness, applicability, implementability, acceptability, originality, paradigm_relatedness\n\nEach with _evidence sentence then score.\n\nLet's produce final answer.",
