gree socially legally politically acceptable. Common strategies that violate no norms... Automated documentation is common and acceptable. Level 4.\n\neffectiveness: degree to which idea will solve problem. Reasonable and will contribute to solution of problem (helps but partial). It addresses logging mandate and record-keeping burden, but does it meet logging mandate? It converts logs to documentation, removes admin burden. The mandate requires automatic recording of events over lifetime. Pipeline helps with documentation but not necessarily ensure logging capabilities for risk situations etc. Might be partial solution. So 3 = reasonable and will contribute to solution, partial.\n\napplicability: degree to which idea clearly applies to stated problem. Solves identified problem directly related to stated problem (do X to get Y, and Y is part of stated problem). Yes, automate compliance documentation from logs for Article 12/Recital 71. So 4.\n\nNeed output format. Probably JSON? Instruction says return rating echoing id exactly. For each subscale give <subscale>_evidence then <subscale>. Could be list.\n\nLet's produce:\n\nid: P2044\noriginality_evidence: ...\noriginality: 2\n...\n\nMake sure one sentence evidence.\n\nLet's craft sentences concise.\n\nOriginality evidence: The pipeline automates conversion of existing logs and version metadata into compliance templates, a practical integration that is interesting but not ingenious or surprising for regulated software teams. Score 2.\n\nParadigm_relatedness evidence: It adds automation tools and processes around logging without changing how R&D relates to compliance obligations, keeping compliance as documentation practice. Score 2.\n\nImplementability evidence: Building scripts that pull from experiment tracking and version control into templates requires some engineering changes but uses existing data sources and CI practices. Score 3.\n\nAcceptability evidence: Automating documentation generation aligns with standard engineering norms and does not violate legal, social or business sensibilities. Score 4.\n\nEffectiveness evidence: The pipeline reduces manual record-keeping burden and produces up-to-date technical documentation, contributing to traceability but not ensuring the mandated logging of risk-relevant events themselves. Score 3.\n\nApplicability evidence: It directly addresses the lab's need to meet Article 12 record-keeping and Recital 71 documentation requirements from automatic logs. Score 4.\n\nReturn.\n\n",
