        "reasoning_content": "You are a rater applying idea-evaluation instrument...\n\nWe need rating for proposal P2682.\n\nProposal: Dynamic Technical Documentation Engine. intelligent tool automatically generates and updates technical documentation by extracting info from code, config files, logging metadata. ensures general characteristics always current. pipeline parses version control commits and runtime performance logs to populate living documents real-time without manual entry. removes admin friction...\n\nWe need six subscales.\n\noriginality evidence sentence then score.\nparadigm_relatedness evidence...\nimplementability\nacceptability\napplicability\neffectiveness\n\nNeed anchored levels exactly.\n\nOriginality (1-4): degree to which idea is not only rare but also ingenious, imaginative, or surprising.\nProposal is dynamic technical doc engine auto generate from code/logs. Is this common? Auto-generating docs from code is fairly common. Using logging metadata to update compliance docs maybe unusual interesting. Probably \"unusual, interesting; shows some imagination\" = 3? Or \"interesting\" =2. Let's think reference class: AI R&D labs responses to logging mandate. Many would propose manual logging, or buy logging tool. Auto doc generation is maybe unusual but not ingenious surprising. Might be 3 = unusual, interesting; shows some imagination. Could be 2=interesting. I'd say 3.\n\nparadigm_relatedness (1-4): degree to which idea preserves or modifies paradigm.\n4 = paradigm breaking: introduces new elements AND changes relationship between lab's R&D and compliance obligations, or addresses larger problem of what lab researches\n3 = paradigm stretching: changes that relationship (e.g., logging woven into science, new partners or offerings born from compliance)\n2 = slightly paradigm stretching: introduces new elements (tools, formats, processes) but still treats compliance as logging practice\n1 = paradigm preserving\n\nThis proposal introduces tool but