actice => 2.\n\nImplementability (1-4): easy to implement at low cost or non-radical changes =4; some changes reasonably feasible=3; significant change expensive/difficult=2; totally infeasible=1\n\nCentralized Python library with wrappers, secure database. Some changes, reasonably feasible. Probably 3 = some changes, or reasonably feasible measures. Could be 4 easy? Might require engineering effort across prototypes. I'd say 3.\n\nAcceptability (1-4): common strategies violate no norms =4; somewhat uncommon...=3; offends sensibilities somewhat=2; radically violates=1\n\nLogging for compliance is legally required, socially acceptable. 4 = common strategies that violate no norms or sensibilities.\n\nEffectiveness (1-4): degree to which idea will solve problem.\n4 reasonable and will solve stated problem without regard workability\n3 reasonable and will contribute...\n2 unreasonable...\n1 solves unrelated...\n\nWill standardized logging ensure Article 12 requirements by default? Captures timestamps, input hashes, system states. Article 12 requires recording events relevant for identifying risk, post-market monitoring, monitoring operation, and for systems in Annex III point 1(a) specific logs: period of use, reference database, input data match, identification of natural persons. Library captures timestamps and hashes but may not capture all required specific items like reference database, identification of persons. But it's a general approach. Probably will contribute but not fully guarantee compliance? It ensures logging by default without manual coding. Might be partial solution. Could be 3 = reasonable and will contribute to solution, only partial.\n\nApplicability (1-4): degree idea clearly applies to stated problem.\n4 solves identified problem directly related\n3 solves implied problem related\n2 may have some benefit...\n1 intervention not stated...\n\nIt directly addresses logging mandate. So 4 = solves an identified problem that is directly related to stated