s logging mandate for R&D, relevant. It applies to record-keeping requirement albeit training phase. Might be 4? It solves identified problem that is directly related: do X to get Y and Y is part of stated problem. Logging during training is part of technical documentation per Recital 71. Article 12 requires automatic recording of events logs over lifetime of system. Training is part of lifetime. So applicability maybe 4.\n\nBut also applicability scale: solves identified problem directly related. Yes logging mandate. I'd say 4.\n\nEvidence: The framework provides automatic event logging for model development, which maps directly to Recital 71's requirement for technical documentation and Article 12's logging mandate.\n\nScore 4.\n\nAcceptability: degree socially legally politically acceptable. Common strategies that violate no norms. Standard software library wrapper is common. Score 4.\n\nEvidence: Automating provenance capture via framework wrappers is a routine engineering practice that complies with EU AI Act requirements and raises no legal or ethical concerns.\n\nScore 4.\n\nImplementability: degree can be easily implemented. Some changes, reasonably feasible measures? Developing standardized library and wrapper for common ML frameworks is some changes, reasonably feasible but not trivial low cost? Might be 3 = some changes, or reasonably feasible measures. Could be 3. Not easy low cost (4). It's significant engineering effort. So 3.\n\nEvidence: Building and maintaining a cross-framework wrapper and provenance library requires dedicated engineering effort and integration work across active projects, which is feasible but not trivially low-cost.\n\nScore 3.\n\nParadigm_relatedness: degree to which idea preserves or modifies paradigm anchored to this problem.\nLevels:\n4 = paradigm breaking: introduces new elements AND changes relationship between lab's R&D and its compliance obligations, or addresses larger problem of what lab researches\n3 = paradigm stretching: changes that relationship (e.g., logging woven into science, new partners or offerings born from compliance)\n2 = slightly paradigm stretching: introduces new elements (tools, formats, processes) but still treats compliance as logging practice\n1 = paradigm preserving: standard compliance and logging practice; lab's way of working is unchanged\n\nProposal introduces standardized software library, wrapper. That's new tools but still treats compliance as logging practice. Might be slightly paradigm stretching: introduces new elements but still treats compliance as logging practice = 2.\n\nEvidence: It adds provenance tooling to research workflows but keeps compliance framed as record-keeping for audits rather than reshaping R&D or compliance relationship.\n\nScore 2.\n\nOriginality: degree rare and ingenious.\n4 = not expressed before AND ingenious...\n3 = unusual, interesting; shows some imagination\n2 = interesting\n1 = common, mundane, boring\n\nUnified research provenance framework with wrappers is fairly common idea in ML reproducibility. Probably common, mundane. Score 1? Could be 2 interesting. Let's think reference class: experienced evaluator expect AI R&D labs to give responses. Provenance logging is common suggestion. Might be common, mundane. So 1.\n\nEvidence: Automated training provenance and framework wrappers are a widely discussed, standard approach for reproducibility and regulatory logging in ML teams.\n\nScore 1.\n\nReturn rating echo id P1380.\n\nFormat: need <subscale>_evidence then <subscale>. Probably lines.\n\nLet's output:\n\nid: P1380\neffectiveness_evidence: ...\neffectiveness: 3\netc.\n\nEnsure one sentence evidence each.\n\nProceed.",
