arch value. That's changing relationship. I'd say paradigm stretching = 3.\n\nOriginality (1-4):\n4 = not expressed before AND ingenious...\n3 = unusual, interesting; shows some imagination\n2 = interesting\n1 = common, mundane\n\nOpen-source provenance framework for high-risk AI capturing hyperparameters etc. Is that common? Provenance frameworks exist (MLflow, Weights & Biases, DVC). Open source provenance for compliance is fairly common/mundane. Might be interesting but not unusual. I'd say 2 = interesting? Or 1 = common, mundane. The description is fairly standard. Could be 2 interesting. Hmm. Probably 2.\n\nEffectiveness (1-4):\n4 = reasonable and will solve stated problem without regard for workability\n3 = reasonable and will contribute...\n2 = unreasonable or unlikely\n1 = solves unrelated\n\nDoes it solve logging mandate? Automatic recording of events logs over lifetime, logging capabilities for traceability... The proposal captures hyperparameters, dataset versions, execution logs throughout development lifecycle. Article 12 requires automatic recording of events (logs) over lifetime of system, recording period of each use etc for certain systems. Development provenance may help but does it meet operational logging requirement? It creates immutable timestamped records of every single training run. Might be partial. Could contribute but not fully solve operational logging for deployed high-risk system. Effectiveness maybe 3 = contributes to solution, partial.\n\nApplicability (1-4):\n4 = solves identified problem directly related\n3 = solves implied problem related\n2 = may have some benefit within special situation...\nProposal clearly applies to logging mandate and compliance. It addresses record-keeping. That's direct. Applicability 4.\n\nImplementability (1-4):\n4 = easy low cost non-radical\n3 = some changes reasonably feasible\n2 = significant change expensive difficult but not impossible\n1 = totally infeasible\n\nIntegrate modular software library i