ements (tools, formats, processes) but still treats compliance as logging practice =2. Could be 2.\n\nOriginality (1-4): degree to which idea is rare but ingenious...\nCommon, mundane boring =1? Unified logging middleware is common in software engineering. Probably common, mundane. So originality 1.\n\nWe need output format: echo id exactly. For each subscale give <subscale>_evidence then <subscale>. Probably JSON? The instruction says return rating... give \"<subscale>_evidence\" first and then \"<subscale>\". Might be plain text.\n\nI'll produce structured output:\n\nid: P2982\napplicability_evidence: ...\napplicability: 4\n...\n\nLet's craft one sentence evidence each.\n\nApplicability evidence: The middleware directly provides automatic event logging required by Article 12 for high-risk AI systems in the lab. Score 4.\n\nEffectiveness evidence: If implemented, it would automatically capture timestamps and system states across all projects, satisfying the core traceability mandate without manual researcher effort. Score 4? Might be 3. Hmm decision.\n\nLet's reconsider effectiveness anchored: 4 = reasonable and will solve the stated problem without regard for workability (if you could do it, it would solve the main problem). Does capturing timestamps and system states automatically ensure recording of events relevant for risk identification, post-market monitoring, etc., and specific items like reference database, input data match? It captures system states, maybe sufficient. Might be partial. I'd go with 3 = reasonable and will contribute to solution of problem (it helps, but only partial). Because it doesn't explicitly guarantee logging of the specific events listed in Article 12(2)-(3). So partial.\n\nImplementability evidence: Building a lightweight wrapper and mandating integration across projects requires engineering effort and researcher adoption but is a standard software practice. Score 3.\n\nAcceptability evidence: Centralized logging for compliance is a