t's choose 3.\n\nOriginality: degree not only rare but ingenious... 4 = not expressed before AND ingenious... 3 = unusual interesting shows some imagination. SDK for compliance decorators is perhaps common idea, not ingenious surprising. Might be interesting but not rare? Probably 2 = interesting. Could be 3 unusual interesting. Let's pick 2? Common mundane boring is 1. It's interesting. I'd say 3 = unusual, interesting; shows some imagination. Possibly 2. Hmm.\n\nParadigm_relatedness: degree preserves or modifies paradigm anchored to problem.\n4 = paradigm breaking: introduces new elements AND changes relationship between lab's R&D and compliance obligations, or addresses larger problem of what lab researches\n3 = paradigm stretching: changes that relationship (e.g., logging woven into science, new partners or offerings born from compliance)\n2 = slightly paradigm stretching: introduces new elements (tools, formats, processes) but still treats compliance as logging practice\n1 = paradigm preserving: standard compliance and logging practice; lab's way unchanged\n\nSDK is tool for logging. Introduces new elements but still treats compliance as logging practice. That's slightly paradigm stretching? It weaves logging into code via decorator, maybe changes relationship. Could be 2. Slightly paradigm stretching: introduces new elements (tools) but still treats compliance as logging practice. So 2.\n\nMaybe originality = 3. Let's decide.\n\nEvidence sentences one sentence each.\n\nReturn rating echoing id.\n\nFormat: Probably JSON? Instruction says give \"<subscale>_evidence\" first then \"<subscale>\". Might output lines.\n\nWe'll produce:\n\nid: P1865\napplicability_evidence: ...\napplicability: 4\n...\n\nLet's do concise one sentence evidence.\n\nEnsure order listed: applicability, effectiveness, acceptability, implementability, originality, paradigm_relatedness\n\nProvide each.\n\nFinal answer.",
