        "reasoning_content": "You are a rater applying idea-evaluation instrument... Apply published anchored levels exactly, one level per subscale, and give one sentence of evidence for each rating.\n\nProposal P2632: Compliance Infrastructure Capital Expenditure Request. dedicated budget request for increased high-durability storage and compute capacity specifically for long-term log retention. Treats compliance as core infrastructure cost. present cost-benefit analysis... ensures regulatory costs do not eat into current project research budgets. secures independent funding for regulatory overhead without sacrificing scientific ambition.\n\nWe need rating six subscales.\n\nparadigm_relatedness (1-4): degree to which idea preserves or modifies paradigm.\nProposal is budget request for storage/compute for logs. That's standard compliance practice; lab way of working unchanged? It treats compliance as core infrastructure cost, independent funding. Does it change relationship between R&D and compliance obligations? Might be slightly paradigm stretching? Introduces new elements (tools/processes) but still treats compliance as logging practice.\n\nDefinition:\n4 = paradigm breaking: introduces new elements AND changes relationship between lab's R&D and its compliance obligations, or addresses larger problem of what lab researches\n3 = paradigm stretching: changes that relationship (e.g., logging woven into science, new partners or offerings born from compliance)\n2 = slightly paradigm stretching: introduces new elements (tools, formats, processes) but still treats compliance as logging practice\n1 = paradigm preserving: standard compliance and logging practice; lab's way of working unchanged\n\nThe proposal is just budget for storage. That's standard compliance. Might be paradigm preserving? It says treats compliance as core infrastructure cost, secures independent funding without sacrificing scientific ambition. That's maybe slightly new element (budgetary process). But still treats compliance as logging practice. Likely 1 or 2.\n\nI think it's paradigm preserving: standard compliance and logging practice; lab's way of working unchanged. It's just asking for money for storage. So 1.\n\nEvidence sentence: It proposes funding for durable log storage, which is conventional record-keeping infrastructure rather than altering R&D-compliance relationship.\n\nOriginality (1-4): degree to which idea is not only rare but ingenious...\n4 = not expressed before AND ingenious\n3 = unusual interesting; shows some imagination\n2 = interesting\n1 = common mundane boring\n\nBudget request for compliance storage is common, mundane. So 1.\n\nEvidence: Requesting dedicated capital for log retention is a routine administrative response that experienced evaluators would expect.\n\nApplicability (1-4): degree to which idea clearly applies to stated problem.\nProblem: AI R&D lab must decide how to respond: how to meet logging mandate and/or adapt R&D accordingly.\nProposal addresses meeting logging mandate via storage capacity. So solves identified problem directly related. 4 = solves an identified problem that is directly related to stated problem (do X to get Y, and Y is part of stated problem). Yes.\n\nEvidence: Securing long-term immutable storage directly enables automatic lifetime log retention required by Article 12(1).\n\nEffectiveness (1-4): degree to which idea will solve problem.\n4 = reasonable and will solve stated problem without regard for workability (if you could do it, it would solve main problem)\nWill storage solve logging mandate? Need also logging capability, not just storage. But storage is necessary component. Might be partial solution. Does it solve main problem? It ensures logs can be retained, but doesn't ensure logs are generated/recorded. However Article 12 requires technically allow automatic recording. Storage is part.\n\nProbably effectiveness 3? Reasonable and will contribute to solution, partial. The mandate includes recording events relevant... storage alone doesn't ensure logging capability. Could argue it would solve retention aspect but not creation. Hmm.\n\nThe anchored: 4 = reasonable and will solve stated problem without regard for workability (if you could do it, it would solve main problem). If you have storage, you still need logging mechanism. So not full solution. Might be 3 contribute.\n\nLet's pick 3.\n\nEvidence: Dedicated storage enables lifetime retention but does not by itself create the required automatic event logging or traceability capabilities.\n\nAcceptability (1-4): degree to which idea is socially legally politically acceptable.\n4 = common strategies that violate no norms\nBudget request is acceptable. So 4.\n\nEvidence: Requesting capital for compliance infrastructure is a normal, non-offensive business practice.\n\nImplementability (1-4): easy to implement at low cost or non-radical changes.\n4 = easy to implement at low cost, or non-radical changes\n3 = some changes, or reasonably feasible measures\n2 = significant change, or expensive or difficult...\n\nDedicated budget request for high-durability storage and compute is not trivial; requires board approval, capital expenditure. Might be reasonably feasible but not easy low cost. Probably 3 = some changes, reasonably feasible.\n\nEvidence: Securing new capital and provisioning durable storage requires board approval and procurement but is a standard IT infrastructure upgrade.\n\nThus ratings:\nparadigm_relatedness 1\noriginality 1\napplicability 4\neffectiveness 3\nacceptability 4\nimplementability 3\n\nReturn JSON? Instructions say return rating echoing its id exactly. For each subscale give <subscale>_evidence then <subscale>. Probably output lines.\n\nLet's produce clear format.\n\n",
