ge for compliance logs is common. Probably common, mundane. Might be interesting but common practice. I'd say 1 = common, mundane, boring.\n\nEvidence: Tiered cold storage for regulatory retention is a standard IT cost-optimization practice widely used in industry.\n\nScore 1.\n\nworkability implementability (1-4)\n4 = easy to implement at low cost, or non-radical changes\n3 = some changes, or reasonably feasible measures\n2 = significant change, or expensive/difficult but not impossible\n1 = totally infeasible\n\nDedicated scalable storage architecture optimized... utilizes existing server capacity more effectively. Seems reasonably feasible. Some changes. Could be 3? Might be easy? It says dedicated scalable storage architecture, tiered system automatically moves older logs. That's some changes, reasonably feasible. Probably 3.\n\nEvidence: Deploying tiered storage using existing servers requires moderate engineering effort but uses established technologies.\n\nScore 3.\n\nacceptability (1-4)\n4 = common strategies that violate no norms\n3 = somewhat uncommon...\n2 = offends sensibilities somewhat...\n1 = radically violates...\n\nSeems acceptable. Common strategies that violate no norms. Score 4.\n\nEvidence: Separating and securely retaining compliance logs aligns with legal requirements and standard data governance norms.\n\nScore 4.\n\nReturn rating echoing id exactly. Provide subscale_evidence then subscale.\n\nFormat? Probably JSON? It says return rating echoing its id exactly. For each subscale give \"<subscale>_evidence\" first and then \"<subscale>\".\n\nProbably output lines.\n\nI'll produce structured list.\n\nLet's output:\n\nid: P1034\napplicability_evidence: ...\napplicability: 4\netc.\n\nEnsure order listed.\n\nProvide one sentence evidence each.\n\nProceed.",
